{"notes":[{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Calibration for Modern AI @ AISTATS 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/6718953efb7dbde7663b9135dff061695a8092d2.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Fairness","Calibration","Bias"]},"venueid":{"value":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI"},"paperhash":{"value":"xu|demographic_calibration_of_visionlanguage_models_for_dermatology"},"authorids":{"value":["~Sonnet_Xu1","~Roxana_Daneshjou1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Vision-language models (VLMs) are increasingly deployed in dermatology, yet their calibration across patient demographics remains understudied. We evaluate GPT-4o on binary skin lesion classification using the Diverse Dermatology Images dataset, finding that standard accuracy (70.1\\%) substantially overstates diagnostic capability relative to balanced accuracy (60.5\\%). Across three confidence extraction methods, we identify a calibration--equity tradeoff: verbalized confidence achieves the best aggregate calibration (ECE = 0.073) but the worst demographic disparity (dark skin 2.6$\\times$ worse than light); self-consistency at high temperature is most equitable (max ECE gap = 0.009) but sacrifices discrimination; token-level probabilities offer the strongest discrimination (AUROC = 0.655) but severe overconfidence (21.5\\% error rate at $>$99\\% confidence). Post-hoc temperature scaling substantially improves token-level calibration (ECE reduced 80--96\\%), yet the equity ranking across signals is preserved after recalibration. These findings show that confidence method selection has direct fairness implications for VLM-based clinical decision support. Code: https://github.com/sonnetx/demographic-calibration-aistats"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nxu2026demographic,\ntitle={Demographic Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Dermatology},\nauthor={Sonnet Xu and Roxana Daneshjou},\nbooktitle={Towards Trustworthy Predictions: Theory and Applications of Calibration for Modern AI},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=AnrhY1hrlH}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Demographic Calibration of Vision-Language Models for Dermatology"},"authors":{"value":["Sonnet Xu","Roxana Daneshjou"]}},"tmdate":1776068199489,"pdate":1776068197433,"tcdate":1771646622841,"writers":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission12/Authors"],"signatures":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission12/Authors"],"forum":"AnrhY1hrlH","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":12,"cdate":1771646622841,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Post_Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission12/-/Revision","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Edit"],"mdate":1776068199489,"odate":1776068197433,"domain":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","id":"AnrhY1hrlH","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICSE 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11029684/11029718/11029728.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICSE/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"spiess|calibration_and_correctness_of_language_models_for_code"},"authorids":{"value":["~Claudio_Spiess1","~David_Gros1","~Kunal_Suresh_Pai1","~Michael_Pradel1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Md._Rafiqul_Islam_Rabin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Amin_Alipour:","~Susmit_Jha1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Prem_Devanbu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Toufique_Ahmed:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSE55347.2025.00040"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icse/Spiess0PPRAJDA25,\n  author={Claudio Spiess and David Gros and Kunal Suresh Pai and Michael Pradel and Md. Rafiqul Islam Rabin and Amin Alipour and Susmit Jha and Prem Devanbu and Toufique Ahmed},\n  title={Calibration and Correctness of Language Models for Code},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={540-552},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/ICSE55347.2025.00040},\n  booktitle={ICSE},\n  crossref={conf/icse/2025}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Machine learning models are widely used, but can also often be wrong. Users would benefit from a reliable indication of whether a given output from a given model should be trusted, so a rational decision can be made whether to use the output or not. For example, outputs can be associated with a confidence measure; if this confidence measure is strongly associated with likelihood of correctness, then the model is said to be well-calibrated. A well-calibrated confidence measure can serve as a basis for rational, graduated decision-making on how much review and care is needed when using generated code. Calibration has so far been studied in mostly non-generative (e.g., classification) settings, especially in software engineering. However, generated code can quite often be wrong: Given generated code, developers must decide whether to use directly, use after varying intensity of careful review, or discard model-generated code. Thus, calibration is vital in generative settings. We make several contributions. We develop a framework for evaluating the calibration of code-generating models. We consider several tasks, correctness criteria, datasets, and approaches, and find that, by and large, generative code models we test are not well-calibrated out of the box. We then show how calibration can be improved using standard methods, such as Platt scaling. Since Platt scaling relies on the prior availability of correctness data, we evaluate the applicability and generalizability of Platt scaling in software engineering, discuss settings where it has good potential for practical use, and settings where it does not. Our contributions will lead to better-calibrated decision-making in the current use of code generated by language models, and offers a framework for future research to further improve calibration methods for generative models in software engineering."},"title":{"value":"Calibration and Correctness of Language Models for Code"},"authors":{"value":["Claudio Spiess","David Gros","Kunal Suresh Pai","Michael Pradel","Md. Rafiqul Islam Rabin","Amin Alipour","Susmit Jha","Prem Devanbu","Toufique Ahmed"]}},"tmdate":1788399890721,"pdate":1735689600000,"tcdate":1751726236067,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Susmit_Jha1"],"forum":"uS1g2crF3k","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":566089,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1788399890721,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"uS1g2crF3k","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2606.31159v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"siddiq|an_empirical_study_of_security_calibration_in_large_language_models_for_code"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.31159"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2606-31159,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Mohammed Latif Siddiq and Md Nafiu Rahman and Joanna C. S. Santos},\n  title={An Empirical Study of Security Calibration in Large Language Models for Code},\n  year={2026},\n  month={June},\n  cdate={1780272000000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2606.31159},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2606.31159}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are rapidly transforming software development, yet their use in security-critical contexts raises a key question: do models know when their generated code is insecure? This property, known as calibration, measures whether a model's confidence aligns with the true correctness of its outputs. We present the first large-scale empirical study of security calibration in LLM-generated code. We evaluate GPT-4o-mini, Gemini-2.0-Flash, and Qwen3-Coder-Next across multiple temperature settings on two complementary benchmarks: self-contained security tasks and multi-language repository-level contexts. Our results suggest that overconfidence is prevalent across the evaluated LLMs. Functional calibration is consistently worse than security calibration, suggesting that models estimate security outcomes more reliably than functional correctness, potentially because functional correctness depends on complex execution behavior. We also examine whether calibration-guided automated repair can help remediate vulnerabilities in LLM-generated code, finding only limited improvements while frequently introducing functional regressions. Moreover, we study different mitigation strategies for reducing False Trust, where models assign high confidence to vulnerable code. The results show that although architectural gating improves calibration on controlled benchmarks, calibration deteriorates in realistic repository-level settings, increasing the risk of high-confidence vulnerable outputs."},"title":{"value":"An Empirical Study of Security Calibration in Large Language Models for Code"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Mohammed Latif Siddiq","username":""},{"fullname":"Md Nafiu Rahman","username":"~Md_Nafiu_Rahman1"},{"fullname":"Joanna C. S. Santos","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1791810678602,"pdate":1798675200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2606-31159"],"tcdate":1791810676626,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Md_Nafiu_Rahman1"],"forum":"aJj2QlNpEd","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":173188,"cdate":1780272000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791810678602,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"aJj2QlNpEd","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We analyze how estimates of calibration shift when applying multi-modal LLMs to generative versus classification tasks"},"venue":{"value":"Calibration for Modern AI @ AISTATS 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/3398826933ee37d46e3a0c35dffc8977517970aa.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Large Language Models","Uncertainty Quantification","Calibration","Multi-Modal"]},"venueid":{"value":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI"},"paperhash":{"value":"kennedy|auditing_the_performance_and_calibration_of_multimodal_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Brendan_Kennedy2","~Lauren_Phillips1","~Sai_Munikoti1","~Sameera_Horawalavithana1","~Ian_Stewart1","~Karl_Pazdernik1"]},"abstract":{"value":"The impressive accuracy scores of Multi-modal Large Language Models (MLLMs) on visual multiple-choice question answering (MCQA) tasks only begins to measure their readiness for sensitive domains such as medicine, scientific research, and multi-modal analytics. Here, we conduct an analysis using uncertainty quantification (UQ) methods for text generation to probe the robustness and calibration underlying the strong performance of MLLMs on image QA benchmarks. Among several findings, we show that model calibration shifts drastically when comparing UQ metrics in the classification versus the open-ended, generative setting increasingly employed by MLLMs. Based on our analysis, we suggest that the path to robust, deployable MLLMs requires not only achieving high accuracy on benchmarks, but also improving performance and calibration on challenging, open-ended tasks across the multi-modal spectrum."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nkennedy2026auditing,\ntitle={Auditing the Performance and Calibration of Multi-Modal Large Language Models},\nauthor={Brendan Kennedy and Lauren Phillips and Sai Munikoti and Sameera Horawalavithana and Ian Stewart and Karl Pazdernik},\nbooktitle={Towards Trustworthy Predictions: Theory and Applications of Calibration for Modern AI},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=GvKCs3aQWz}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Auditing the Performance and Calibration of Multi-Modal Large Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Brendan Kennedy","Lauren Phillips","Sai Munikoti","Sameera Horawalavithana","Ian Stewart","Karl Pazdernik"]}},"tmdate":1776068201577,"pdate":1776068201487,"tcdate":1772228695823,"writers":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission24/Authors"],"signatures":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission24/Authors"],"forum":"GvKCs3aQWz","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":24,"cdate":1772228695823,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Post_Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission24/-/Revision","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Edit"],"mdate":1776068201577,"odate":1776068201487,"domain":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","id":"GvKCs3aQWz","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ICSE 2024"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/ICSE/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"zhou|on_calibration_of_pretrained_code_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Zhenhao_Zhou1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Chaofeng_Sha:","~Xin_Peng6"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3639126"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/icse/ZhouS024,\n  author={Zhenhao Zhou and Chaofeng Sha and Xin Peng},\n  title={On Calibration of Pre-trained Code Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  pages={77:1-77:13},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3597503.3639126},\n  booktitle={ICSE},\n  crossref={conf/icse/2024}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Pre-trained code models have achieved notable success in the field of Software Engineering (SE). However, existing studies have predominantly focused on improving model performance, with limited attention given to other critical aspects such as model calibration. Model calibration, which refers to the accurate estimation of predictive uncertainty, is a vital consideration in practical applications. Therefore, in order to advance the understanding of model calibration in SE, we conduct a comprehensive investigation into the calibration of pre-trained code models in this paper. Our investigation focuses on five pre-trained code models and four code understanding tasks, including analyses of calibration in both in-distribution and out-of-distribution settings. Several key insights are uncovered: (1) pre-trained code models may suffer from the issue of over-confidence; (2) temperature scaling and label smoothing are effective in calibrating code models in in-distribution data; (3) the issue of over-confidence in pre-trained code models worsens in different out-of-distribution settings, and the effectiveness of temperature scaling and label smoothing diminishes. All materials used in our experiments are available at https://github.com/queserasera22/Calibration-of-Pretrained-Code-Models."},"title":{"value":"On Calibration of Pre-trained Code Models"},"authors":{"value":["Zhenhao Zhou","Chaofeng Sha","Xin Peng"]}},"tmdate":1747306386933,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1731477758025,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Xin_Peng6"],"forum":"c0Hhw7zjbi","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":204589,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747306386933,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"c0Hhw7zjbi","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We derive the maximum number of PIT histogram bins for calibration testing that balances discretization bias vs. statistical noise, and provide a minimax optimal test."},"venue":{"value":"Calibration for Modern AI @ AISTATS 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/018fafd1accc5b2af40011ebc3c0b44a7ce9d730.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Calibration testing","Probability Integral Transform (PIT)","Minimax optimality","Uniformity testing","Histogram binning"]},"venueid":{"value":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI"},"paperhash":{"value":"kipnis|calibrating_the_calibration_tester_optimal_binning_and_minimax_calibration_testing_for_continuous_predictive_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Alon_Kipnis1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Evaluating the calibration of continuous predictive models relies heavily on the binned Probability Integral Transform (PIT). Practitioners routinely use arbitrary bin counts ($N$) and standard $\\chi^2$ goodness-of-fit tests, which lack theoretical guarantees against worst-case calibration errors, especially in the large-$N$ regime or when the sample size fluctuates. In this work, we translate recent advances in minimax uniformity testing to the machine learning calibration setting. By mapping regression Expected Calibration Error ($\\ECE$) to the $\\ell_p$ distance from uniformity, we provide two rigorous tools for practitioners: (1) a formula for the maximum allowable bins $N_{\\text{max}}$ to guarantee detection of a target $\\ECE$, and (2) a test that achieves minimax optimal rates. Finally, we discuss the trade-off between discretization bias and statistical noise, and demonstrate how the formula for $N_{\\text{max}}$ provides a principled way to choose $N$ that balances these two effects."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nkipnis2026calibrating,\ntitle={Calibrating the Calibration Tester: Optimal Binning and Minimax Calibration Testing for Continuous Predictive Models},\nauthor={Alon Kipnis},\nbooktitle={Towards Trustworthy Predictions: Theory and Applications of Calibration for Modern AI},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=dy7XNC3W0g}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Calibrating the Calibration Tester: Optimal Binning and Minimax Calibration Testing for Continuous Predictive Models"},"authors":{"value":["Alon Kipnis"]}},"tmdate":1776068201686,"pdate":1776068201626,"tcdate":1772275362842,"writers":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission31/Authors"],"signatures":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission31/Authors"],"forum":"dy7XNC3W0g","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":31,"cdate":1772275362842,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Post_Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission31/-/Revision","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Edit"],"mdate":1776068201686,"odate":1776068201626,"domain":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","id":"dy7XNC3W0g","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MoDELS 2013"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-41533-3_17.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"cazzola|finegrained_software_evolution_using_uml_activity_and_class_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Walter_Cazzola1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nicole_Alicia_Rossini:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Mohammed_Al-Refai:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Robert_B._France:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-41533-3_17"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/CazzolaRAF13,\n  author={Walter Cazzola and Nicole Alicia Rossini and Mohammed Al-Refai and Robert B. France},\n  title={Fine-Grained Software Evolution Using UML Activity and Class Models},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  pages={271-286},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-41533-3_17},\n  booktitle={MoDELS},\n  crossref={conf/models/2013}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Modern software systems that play critical roles in society’s infrastructures are often required to change at runtime so that they can continuously provide essential services in the dynamic environments they operate in. Updating open, distributed software systems at runtime is very challenging. Using runtime models as an interface for updating software at runtime can help developers manage the complexity of updating software while it is executing. In this work we describe an approach to updating Java software at runtime through the use of runtime models consisting of UML class and activity diagrams. Changes to models are turned into changes on Java source code, which is then propagated to the runtime system using the JavAdaptor technology. In particular, the presented approach permits in-the-small software changes, i.e., changes at the code statement level, as opposed to in-the-large changes, i.e., changes at the component level. We present a case study that demonstrates the major aspects of the approach and its use."},"title":{"value":"Fine-Grained Software Evolution Using UML Activity and Class Models"},"authors":{"value":["Walter Cazzola","Nicole Alicia Rossini","Mohammed Al-Refai","Robert B. France"]}},"tmdate":1747344684862,"pdate":1356998400000,"tcdate":1747344628837,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Walter_Cazzola1"],"forum":"xxbsy0dBmG","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":498879,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747344684862,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xxbsy0dBmG","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MoDELS (Companion) 2021"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/9643592/9643613/09643825.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2021"},"paperhash":{"value":"stefanakos|software_performance_engineering_with_performance_antipatterns_and_codelevel_probabilistic_analysis"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ioannis_Stefanakos:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Simos_Gerasimou:","~Radu_Calinescu1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/MODELS-C53483.2021.00045"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/StefanakosGC21,\n  author={Ioannis Stefanakos and Simos Gerasimou and Radu Calinescu},\n  title={Software Performance Engineering with Performance Antipatterns and Code-level Probabilistic Analysis},\n  year={2021},\n  cdate={1609459200000},\n  pages={249-253},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/MODELS-C53483.2021.00045},\n  booktitle={MoDELS (Companion)},\n  crossref={conf/models/2021c}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present the integration of two performance analysis and refactoring techniques based on probabilistic modelling and performance antipatterns. The integrated approach obtained in this way allows software engineers to verify performance properties at both code-level and system architecture-level, supporting the selection of refactoring actions when a performance requirement violation occurs. Monitoring the effect of changes in the system’s code and architecture gives a better overview of (and control over) the system’s performance objectives. We demonstrate the application of the approach in a case study involving the performance analysis and refactoring of a foreign exchange service-based system that comprises a combination of internal components and third-party services."},"title":{"value":"Software Performance Engineering with Performance Antipatterns and Code-level Probabilistic Analysis"},"authors":{"value":["Ioannis Stefanakos","Simos Gerasimou","Radu Calinescu"]}},"tmdate":1738096082265,"pdate":1609459200000,"tcdate":1737848759413,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Radu_Calinescu1"],"forum":"lr8tTFMB3S","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":279086,"cdate":1609459200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1738096082265,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"lr8tTFMB3S","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MoDELS 2009"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-04425-0_25.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2009"},"paperhash":{"value":"milanovic|ruleenhanced_business_process_modeling_language_for_service_choreographies"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Milan_Milanovic:","~Dragan_Gasevic1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Gerd_Wagner_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marek_Hatala:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-04425-0_25"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/MilanovicGWH09,\n  author={Milan Milanovic and Dragan Gasevic and Gerd Wagner and Marek Hatala},\n  title={Rule-Enhanced Business Process Modeling Language for Service Choreographies},\n  year={2009},\n  cdate={1230768000000},\n  pages={337-341},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-04425-0_25},\n  booktitle={MoDELS},\n  crossref={conf/models/2009}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"To address problem of modeling service choreographies, the paper tackles the following challenges of the state of the art in choreography modeling: i) choreography models are not well-connected with the underlying business vocabulary models. ii) there is limited support for decoupling parts of business logic from complete choreography models. This reduces dynamic changes of choreographies; iii) choreography models contain redundant elements of shared business logic, which might lead to an inconsistent implementation and incompatible behavior. Our proposal – rBPMN – is an extension of a business process modeling language with rule and choreography modeling support. rBPMN is defined by weaving the metamodels of the Business Process Modeling Notation (BPMN) and REWERSE Rule Markup Language (R2ML)."},"title":{"value":"Rule-Enhanced Business Process Modeling Language for Service Choreographies"},"authors":{"value":["Milan Milanovic","Dragan Gasevic","Gerd Wagner","Marek Hatala"]}},"tmdate":1718683375076,"pdate":1230768000000,"tcdate":1718683267638,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dragan_Gasevic1"],"forum":"SaNxov3JoB","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":32876,"cdate":1230768000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1718683375076,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"SaNxov3JoB","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"An extension of a variational calibration error estimator that was introduced for proper calibration errors to a large class of non-proper calibration errors, induced by $L_p$ divergences."},"venue":{"value":"Calibration for Modern AI @ AISTATS 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/cb7465a3285a421f05c3e06cc99d39adf848ffbd.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["Calibration","Calibration error","Classification"]},"venueid":{"value":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI"},"paperhash":{"value":"berta|a_variational_estimator_for_l_p_calibration_errors"},"authorids":{"value":["~Eugène_Berta1","~Sacha_Braun1","~David_Holzmüller1","~Michael_I._Jordan2","~Francis_Bach1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Calibration—the problem of ensuring that predicted probabilities align with observed class frequencies—is a basic desideratum for reliable prediction with machine learning systems. Calibration error is traditionally assessed via a divergence function, using the expected divergence between predictions and empirical frequencies. Accurately estimating this quantity is challenging, especially in the multiclass setting. Here, we show how to extend a recent variational framework for estimating calibration errors beyond divergences induced induced by proper losses, to cover a broad class of calibration errors induced by $L_p$ divergences. Our method can separate over- and under-confidence and, unlike non-variational approaches, avoids overestimation. We provide extensive experiments and integrate our code in the open-source package probmetrics (https://github.com/dholzmueller/probmetrics) for evaluating calibration errors."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nberta2026a,\ntitle={A Variational Estimator for \\$L\\_p\\$ Calibration Errors},\nauthor={Eug{\\`e}ne Berta and Sacha Braun and David Holzm{\\\"u}ller and Michael I. Jordan and Francis Bach},\nbooktitle={Towards Trustworthy Predictions: Theory and Applications of Calibration for Modern AI},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=mrR4eS2nlA}\n}"},"title":{"value":"A Variational Estimator for $L_p$ Calibration Errors"},"authors":{"value":["Eugène Berta","Sacha Braun","David Holzmüller","Michael I. Jordan","Francis Bach"]}},"tmdate":1776068198053,"pdate":1776068197446,"tcdate":1772123210515,"writers":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission19/Authors"],"signatures":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission19/Authors"],"forum":"mrR4eS2nlA","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":19,"cdate":1772123210515,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Post_Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission19/-/Revision","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Edit"],"mdate":1776068198053,"odate":1776068197446,"domain":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","id":"mrR4eS2nlA","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MoDELS 2011"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-24485-8_39.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2011"},"paperhash":{"value":"stenzel|formal_verification_of_qvt_transformations_for_code_generation"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kurt_Stenzel:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Nina_Moebius:","~Wolfgang_Reif1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-24485-8_39"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/StenzelMR11,\n  author={Kurt Stenzel and Nina Moebius and Wolfgang Reif},\n  title={Formal Verification of QVT Transformations for Code Generation},\n  year={2011},\n  cdate={1293840000000},\n  pages={533-547},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-24485-8_39},\n  booktitle={MoDELS},\n  crossref={conf/models/2011}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"We present a formal calculus for operational QVT. The calculus is implemented in the interactive theorem prover KIV and allows to prove properties of QVT transformations for arbitrary meta models."},"title":{"value":"Formal Verification of QVT Transformations for Code Generation"},"authors":{"value":["Kurt Stenzel","Nina Moebius","Wolfgang Reif"]}},"tmdate":1731171482195,"pdate":1293840000000,"tcdate":1731171376931,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Wolfgang_Reif1"],"forum":"Bl1UAEcYr3","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":177153,"cdate":1293840000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1731171482195,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Bl1UAEcYr3","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"We introduce a vector-quantized, region-aware extension of Dirichlet Calibration that achieves strong local calibration results while preserving global calibration and predictive performance through a parameter-efficient design."},"venue":{"value":"Calibration for Modern AI @ AISTATS 2026"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/f631d20e1017a475322f966d5e91d73c9a0d1230.pdf"},"keywords":{"value":["calibration","local-calibration","representation-learning"]},"venueid":{"value":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI"},"paperhash":{"value":"barbera|dirichlet_calibration_goes_local"},"authorids":{"value":["~Cesare_Barbera1","~Lorenzo_Perini1","~Giovanni_De_Toni1","~Andrea_Passerini2","~Andrea_Pugnana1"]},"abstract":{"value":"Accurate and well-calibrated Machine Learning (ML) models are mandatory in high-stakes settings, yet effective calibration remains challenging: global approaches assume calibration errors are homogeneous across the space, while local methods often rely on latent-space dimensionality reduction, which leads to information loss. We address these issues by injecting a notion of locality in Dirichlet Calibration via Vector Quantization (VQ).  We further introduce an efficient parametrization of the Dirichlet concentrations that prevents a combinatorial explosion of calibration parameters.\nOur approach allows us to learn heterogeneous calibration maps that generalize well even to sparse regions of the latent space. Experiments on benchmark datasets show significant improvements in local calibration while preserving global calibration and predictive performance."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nbarbera2026dirichlet,\ntitle={Dirichlet Calibration Goes Local},\nauthor={Cesare Barbera and Lorenzo Perini and Giovanni De Toni and Andrea Passerini and Andrea Pugnana},\nbooktitle={Towards Trustworthy Predictions: Theory and Applications of Calibration for Modern AI},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=DD9EjE3AKD}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Dirichlet Calibration Goes Local"},"authors":{"value":["Cesare Barbera","Lorenzo Perini","Giovanni De Toni","Andrea Passerini","Andrea Pugnana"]}},"tmdate":1776068198042,"pdate":1776068197440,"tcdate":1772008101696,"writers":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission16/Authors"],"signatures":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission16/Authors"],"forum":"DD9EjE3AKD","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":16,"cdate":1772008101696,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Post_Submission","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/Submission16/-/Revision","aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI/-/Edit"],"mdate":1776068198042,"odate":1776068197440,"domain":"aistats.org/AISTATS/2026/Workshop/Calibration_for_Modern_AI","id":"DD9EjE3AKD","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MODELS-C 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel8/11273081/11273207/11273233.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"zou|automated_aadl_architecture_modeling_leveraging_large_language_models_for_safetycritical_software"},"authorids":{"value":["","","","","","~Zonghua_Gu1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/MODELS-C68889.2025.00050"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/ZouYLLZG25,\n  author={Yaxin Zou and Zhibin Yang and Hao Liu and Jiawei Liang and Yong Zhou and Zonghua Gu},\n  title={Automated AADL Architecture Modeling: Leveraging Large Language Models for Safety-Critical Software},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={311-321},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/MODELS-C68889.2025.00050},\n  booktitle={MODELS-C},\n  crossref={conf/models/2025c}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Architecture modeling is an essential part of model-driven development of safety-critical software. AADL is a modeling language standard for designing and analyzing safety-critical software. However, there is usually a large gap between software requirements and architectural design. Effectively transforming requirements into formal software architecture models relies on a lot of manual experience and iterative exploration. To address these challenges, we conduct an exploratory study of LLM-supported AADL architecture modeling. We assess three powerful LLMs, GPT-4o, DeepSeekV3, and GLM-4-Plus. First, we decompose and refine high-level software requirements and design constraints, mapping them to the proposed prompt engineering framework, RNL-Prompt, which significantly improved the accuracy of LLMs in generating different modeling elements. Our findings reveal that GPT-4o and DeepSeek-V3 perform on par with each other, and both outperform GLM-4-Plus in complex modeling elements, such as modes, behavior annex, etc. Second, to enhance the potential of GLM-4-Plus, we optimize its performance using N-shot prompting and retrievalaugmented generation (RAG). The results indicate that N-shot prompting performs more effectively. Finally, we demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed approach in generating AADL architecture models in five examples of safety-critical domains. In addition, we implement an LLM-based modeling tool based on the AADL open source environment OSATE, which supports GPT-4o, DeepSeek-V3, and GLM-4-Plus. The tool is successfully applied to the modeling of an avionics control system in the industry."},"title":{"value":"Automated AADL Architecture Modeling: Leveraging Large Language Models for Safety-Critical Software"},"authors":{"value":["Yaxin Zou","Zhibin Yang","Hao Liu","Jiawei Liang","Yong Zhou","Zonghua Gu"]}},"tmdate":1772162214371,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/models/ZouYLLZG25"],"tcdate":1772162209174,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Zonghua_Gu1"],"forum":"HCsbHuEEj7","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":831076,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1772162214371,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"HCsbHuEEj7","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.04655v4"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"wang|openvocabulary_calibration_for_visionlanguage_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shuoyuan_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jindong_Wang_0005:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Guoqing_Wang:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Bob_Zhang_0001:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Kaiyang_Zhou:","~Hongxin_Wei1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.04655"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2402-04655,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Shuoyuan Wang and Jindong Wang and Guoqing Wang and Bob Zhang and Kaiyang Zhou and Hongxin Wei},\n  title={Open-Vocabulary Calibration for Vision-Language Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2402.04655},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2402.04655}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Vision-language models (VLMs) have emerged as formidable tools, showing their strong capability in handling various open-vocabulary tasks in image recognition, text-driven visual content generation, and visual chatbots, to name a few. In recent years, considerable efforts and resources have been devoted to adaptation methods for improving downstream performance of VLMs, particularly on parameter-efficient fine-tuning methods like prompt learning. However, a crucial aspect that has been largely overlooked is the confidence calibration problem in fine-tuned VLMs, which could greatly reduce reliability when deploying such models in the real world. This paper bridges the gap by systematically investigating the confidence calibration problem in the context of prompt learning and reveals that existing calibration methods are insufficient to address the problem, especially in the open-vocabulary setting. To solve the problem, we present a simple and effective approach called Distance-Aware Calibration (DAC), which is based on scaling the temperature using as guidance the distance between predicted text labels and base classes. The experiments with 7 distinct prompt learning methods applied across 11 diverse downstream datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of DAC, which achieves high efficacy without sacrificing the inference speed. Our code is available at https://github.com/ml-stat-Sustech/CLIP_Calibration."},"title":{"value":"Open-Vocabulary Calibration for Vision-Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Shuoyuan Wang","Jindong Wang","Guoqing Wang","Bob Zhang","Kaiyang Zhou","Hongxin Wei"]}},"tmdate":1767786889146,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2402-04655"],"tcdate":1767786880124,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Hongxin_Wei1"],"forum":"6rsuroQ9Je","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":725789,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1767786889146,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"6rsuroQ9Je","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.22614v1"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2025"},"paperhash":{"value":"koohestani|does_inide_calibration_of_large_language_models_work_at_scale"},"authorids":{"value":["~Roham_Koohestani1","~Agnia_Sergeyuk1","~David_Gros1","~Claudio_Spiess1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sergey_Titov:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Prem_Devanbu:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Maliheh_Izadi:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.22614"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2510-22614,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Roham Koohestani and Agnia Sergeyuk and David Gros and Claudio Spiess and Sergey Titov and Prem Devanbu and Maliheh Izadi},\n  title={Does In-IDE Calibration of Large Language Models work at Scale?},\n  year={2025},\n  month={October},\n  cdate={1759276800000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2510.22614},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.22614}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The introduction of large language models into integrated development environments (IDEs) is revolutionizing software engineering, yet it poses challenges to the usefulness and reliability of Artificial Intelligence-generated code. Post-hoc calibration of internal model confidences aims to align probabilities with an acceptability measure. Prior work suggests calibration can improve alignment, but at-scale evidence is limited. In this work, we investigate the feasibility of applying calibration of code models to an in-IDE context. We study two aspects of the problem: (1) the technical method for implementing confidence calibration and improving the reliability of code generation models, and (2) the human-centered design principles for effectively communicating reliability signal to developers. First, we develop a scalable and flexible calibration framework which can be used to obtain calibration weights for open-source models using any dataset, and evaluate whether calibrators improve the alignment between model confidence and developer acceptance behavior. Through a large-scale analysis of over 24 million real-world developer interactions across multiple programming languages, we find that a general, post-hoc calibration model based on Platt-scaling does not, on average, improve the reliability of model confidence signals. We also find that while dynamically personalizing calibration to individual users can be effective, its effectiveness is highly dependent on the volume of user interaction data. Second, we conduct a multi-phase design study with 3 expert designers and 153 professional developers, combining scenario-based design, semi-structured interviews, and survey validation, revealing a clear preference for presenting reliability signals via non-numerical, color-coded indicators within the in-editor code generation workflow."},"title":{"value":"Does In-IDE Calibration of Large Language Models work at Scale?"},"authors":{"value":["Roham Koohestani","Agnia Sergeyuk","David Gros","Claudio Spiess","Sergey Titov","Prem Devanbu","Maliheh Izadi"]}},"tmdate":1791187994306,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2510-22614"],"tcdate":1769457509412,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Claudio_Spiess1"],"forum":"Q6XdKPTHDK","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":803926,"cdate":1759276800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1791187994306,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"Q6XdKPTHDK","version":2},{"content":{"TLDR":{"value":"Calibration Fine-Tuning teaches language models to sample from target distributions and transfers beyond mathematical sampling to broader stochastic generation tasks."},"venue":{"value":"ICLR 2027 Conference Submission"},"keywords":{"value":["LLM","randomness","probabilistic calibration","distributional fine-tuning","output diversity","mode collapse","controllable generation"]},"primary_area":{"value":"foundation or frontier models, including LLMs"},"abstract":{"value":"Language models are increasingly used in settings where outputs should follow an intended distribution, yet their generation probabilities are often poorly calibrated to it. In many such requests the distribution is implicit and the valid outputs cannot be listed in advance, so the task cannot be passed to an external random number generator. We study whether this capability can be improved directly through fine-tuning. Concretely, we fine-tune language models on synthetic prompts that require sampling from mathematical distributions, and compare two Calibration Fine-Tuning variants: a soft-target method that converts the desired output distribution into trie-derived next-token targets, and a hard-target method that trains on sampled completions from the same target distribution. Across 12 models spanning four families, both methods substantially improve structured-sampling fidelity on held-out distribution families and unseen parameter settings, showing that training on cheap synthetic mathematical distributions improves sampling on unseen distributions, even in natural language. The gains also transfer to broader stochastic generation benchmarks, including open-ended random generation, where fine-tuned models broaden their first-token support by one to two orders of magnitude, and NoveltyBench. The gains sometimes reduce downstream capability, especially arithmetic reasoning, with costs varying by model. Overall, our results show that probabilistic calibration can be improved through fine-tuning with either objective, and that calibration learned on mathematical distributions transfers to natural-language stochastic generation. Code is available at https://anonymous.4open.science/r/calibration-finetuning-3C11."},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{\nanonymous2026probabilistic,\ntitle={Probabilistic Calibration is a Trainable Capability in Language Models},\nauthor={Anonymous},\nbooktitle={Submitted to The Fifteenth International Conference on Learning Representations},\nyear={2026},\nurl={https://openreview.net/forum?id=vW2cBJfvtF},\nnote={under review}\n}"},"title":{"value":"Probabilistic Calibration is a Trainable Capability in Language Models"},"pdf":{"value":"/pdf/a92a7db4c1fd3c2d1c3ac5730ffe4f5167751862.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission"}},"tmdate":1791235367511,"tcdate":1789809719912,"writers":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission58582/Authors"],"signatures":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission58582/Authors"],"forum":"vW2cBJfvtF","license":"CC BY 4.0","number":58582,"cdate":1789809719912,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Bidding","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/Submission58582/-/Full_Submission","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Submission_Change_Before_Reviewing","ICLR.cc/2027/Conference/-/Edit"],"mdate":1791235367511,"odate":1791031328286,"domain":"ICLR.cc/2027/Conference","id":"vW2cBJfvtF","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"ACL (Findings) 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.362.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"li|task_calibration_calibrating_large_language_models_on_inference_tasks"},"html":{"value":"https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.362/"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/acl/LiLXZ25,\n  author={Yingjie Li and Yun Luo and Xiaotian Xie and Yue Zhang},\n  title={Task Calibration: Calibrating Large Language Models on Inference Tasks},\n  year={2025},\n  cdate={1735689600000},\n  pages={6937-6951},\n  url={https://aclanthology.org/2025.findings-acl.362/},\n  booktitle={ACL (Findings)},\n  crossref={conf/acl/2025f}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large language models (LLMs) have exhibited impressive zero-shot performance on inference tasks. However, LLMs may suffer from spurious correlations between input texts and output labels, which limits LLMs’ ability to reason based purely on general language understanding. For example, in the natural language inference (NLI) task, LLMs may make predictions primarily based on premise or hypothesis, rather than both components. To address this problem that may lead to unexpected performance degradation, we propose task calibration (TC), a zero-shot and inference-only calibration method inspired by mutual information which recovers LLM performance through task reformulation. In NLI, TC encourages LLMs to reason based on both premise and hypothesis, while mitigating the models’ over-reliance on individual premise or hypothesis for inference. Experimental results show that TC achieves a substantial improvement on 13 different benchmarks in the zero-shot setup. We further validate the effectiveness of TC in few-shot setups and various natural language understanding tasks. Further analysis indicates that TC is also robust to prompt templates and has the potential to be integrated with other calibration methods. We publicly release our code to facilitate future research."},"title":{"value":"Task Calibration: Calibrating Large Language Models on Inference Tasks"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"Yingjie Li","username":""},{"fullname":"Yun Luo","username":"~Yun_Luo1"},{"fullname":"Xiaotian Xie","username":""},{"fullname":"Yue Zhang","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1780123313341,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/acl/LiLXZ25"],"tcdate":1780123310295,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~Yun_Luo1"],"forum":"WeiVGc9mcC","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":39042,"cdate":1735689600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1780123313341,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"WeiVGc9mcC","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"Trans. Assoc. Comput. Linguistics 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"https://direct.mit.edu/tacl/article-pdf/doi/10.1162/tacl_a_00705/2474777/tacl_a_00705.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/TACL/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"ni|_evaluating_languagetocode_generation_capabilities_of_large_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ansong_Ni:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Pengcheng_Yin:","~Yilun_Zhao1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Martin_Riddell:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Troy_Feng:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Rui_Shen:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Stephen_Yin:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ye_Liu_0006:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Semih_Yavuz:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Caiming_Xiong:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Shafiq_Joty:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Yingbo_Zhou:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Dragomir_Radev:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Arman_Cohan:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1162/tacl_a_00705"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/tacl/NiYZRFSYLYXJZRCC24,\n  author={Ansong Ni and Pengcheng Yin and Yilun Zhao and Martin Riddell and Troy Feng and Rui Shen and Stephen Yin and Ye Liu and Semih Yavuz and Caiming Xiong and Shafiq Joty and Yingbo Zhou and Dragomir Radev and Arman Cohan},\n  title={: Evaluating Language-to-Code Generation Capabilities of Large Language Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={Trans. Assoc. Comput. Linguistics},\n  volume={12},\n  pages={1311-1329},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1162/tacl_a_00705}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recently, large language models (LLMs), especially those that are pretrained on code, have demonstrated strong capabilities in generating programs from natural language inputs. Despite promising results, there is a notable lack of a comprehensive evaluation of these models’ language-to-code generation capabilities. Existing studies often focus on specific tasks, model architectures, or learning paradigms, leading to a fragmented understanding of the overall landscape. In this work, we present L2CEval, a systematic evaluation of the language-to-code generation capabilities of LLMs on 7 tasks across the domain spectrum of semantic parsing, math reasoning, and Python programming, analyzing the factors that potentially affect their performance, such as model size, pretraining data, instruction tuning, and different prompting methods. In addition, we assess confidence calibration, and conduct human evaluations to identify typical failures across different tasks and models. L2CEval offers a comprehensive understanding of the capabilities and limitations of LLMs in language-to-code generation. We release the evaluation framework1 and all model outputs, hoping to lay the groundwork for further future research.All future evaluations (e.g., LLaMA-3, StarCoder2, etc) will be updated on the project website: https://l2c-eval.github.io/."},"title":{"value":": Evaluating Language-to-Code Generation Capabilities of Large Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Ansong Ni","Pengcheng Yin","Yilun Zhao","Martin Riddell","Troy Feng","Rui Shen","Stephen Yin","Ye Liu","Semih Yavuz","Caiming Xiong","Shafiq Joty","Yingbo Zhou","Dragomir Radev","Arman Cohan"]}},"tmdate":1753907527667,"pdate":1704067200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/tacl/NiYZRFSYLYXJZRCC24"],"tcdate":1753907493829,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Yilun_Zhao1"],"forum":"bKbKS4Yk1C","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":602377,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1753907527667,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"bKbKS4Yk1C","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MoDELS 2013"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-41533-3_38.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2013"},"paperhash":{"value":"sunkle|analyzing_enterprise_models_using_enterprise_architecturebased_ontology"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sagar_Sunkle:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Vinay_Kulkarni_0001:","~Suman_Roychoudhury1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-41533-3_38"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/SunkleKR13,\n  author={Sagar Sunkle and Vinay Kulkarni and Suman Roychoudhury},\n  title={Analyzing Enterprise Models Using Enterprise Architecture-Based Ontology},\n  year={2013},\n  cdate={1356998400000},\n  pages={622-638},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-41533-3_38},\n  booktitle={MoDELS},\n  crossref={conf/models/2013}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Development and maintenance of enterprise systems is becoming more difficult due to change drivers along multiple interconnected dimensions. It is advisable to model the enterprise first and analyze it for potential concerns. For modeling enterprises, ontologies have been considered apt and have been used in the past for the same, but application of ontologies for EA analysis based on concepts of enterprise and relations between them have been scarce. We present our ongoing work on analyzing enterprise models using EA-based ontological representation of enterprise. Our contributions are twofold: first, we show how an existing EA modeling language can be leveraged to create EA ontology and second, we show how two known EA analyses can be realized using this ontology. Initial results suggest that ontology representation facilitates basic EA analysis prototyping due to right mix of representation and inference functionalities and is extensible for more involved EA analyses."},"title":{"value":"Analyzing Enterprise Models Using Enterprise Architecture-Based Ontology"},"authors":{"value":["Sagar Sunkle","Vinay Kulkarni","Suman Roychoudhury"]}},"tmdate":1759653637609,"pdate":1356998400000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/models/SunkleKR13"],"tcdate":1759653601747,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Suman_Roychoudhury1"],"forum":"ZsJx1h9oTn","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":636092,"cdate":1356998400000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1759653637609,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"ZsJx1h9oTn","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MoDELS (Workshops) 2007"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-540-69073-3_4.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2007"},"paperhash":{"value":"favre|4th_international_workshop_on_language_engineering_atem_2007"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Jean-Marie_Favre:","~Dragan_Gasevic1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Ralf_Lämmel:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Andreas_Winter_0001:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-69073-3_4"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/FavreGLW07,\n  author={Jean-Marie Favre and Dragan Gasevic and Ralf Lämmel and Andreas Winter},\n  title={4th International Workshop on Language Engineering (ATEM 2007)},\n  year={2007},\n  cdate={1167609600000},\n  pages={28-33},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-69073-3_4},\n  booktitle={MoDELS (Workshops)},\n  crossref={conf/models/2007w}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Following the great success of previous editions, ATEM2007 is the 4th edition of the ATEM workshop series. The first two editions were held with WCRE in 2003 and 2004, while the 3rd one was held with MoDELS 2006. ATEM has always been focused on engineering of language descriptions. In order to cover as many aspects of language descriptions important for greater success and adoption of model-driven engineering, ATEM has been evolving so as its scope:"},"title":{"value":"4th International Workshop on Language Engineering (ATEM 2007)"},"authors":{"value":["Jean-Marie Favre","Dragan Gasevic","Ralf Lämmel","Andreas Winter"]}},"tmdate":1718683390473,"pdate":1167609600000,"tcdate":1718683269178,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Dragan_Gasevic1"],"forum":"YGaXrCRkRj","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":32921,"cdate":1167609600000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1718683390473,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"YGaXrCRkRj","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MoDELS 2019"},"pdf":{"value":"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/iel7/8894013/8906890/08906971.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2019"},"paperhash":{"value":"burgueño|an_lstmbased_neural_network_architecture_for_model_transformations"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Loli_Burgueño:","~Jordi_Cabot1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Sébastien_Gérard:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1109/MODELS.2019.00013"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/BurguenoCG19,\n  author={Loli Burgueño and Jordi Cabot and Sébastien Gérard},\n  title={An LSTM-Based Neural Network Architecture for Model Transformations},\n  year={2019},\n  cdate={1546300800000},\n  pages={294-299},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1109/MODELS.2019.00013},\n  booktitle={MoDELS},\n  crossref={conf/models/2019}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Model transformations are a key element in any model-driven engineering approach. But writing them is a time-consuming and error-prone activity that requires specific knowledge of the transformation language semantics. We propose to take advantage of the advances in Artificial Intelligence and, in particular Long Short-Term Memory Neural Networks (LSTM), to automatically infer model transformations from sets of input-output model pairs. Once the transformation mappings have been learned, the LSTM system is able to autonomously transform new input models into their corresponding output models without the need of writing any transformation-specific code. We evaluate the correctness and performance of our approach and discuss its advantages and limitations."},"title":{"value":"An LSTM-Based Neural Network Architecture for Model Transformations"},"authors":{"value":["Loli Burgueño","Jordi Cabot","Sébastien Gérard"]}},"tmdate":1747574242188,"pdate":1546300800000,"tcdate":1747574214138,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jordi_Cabot1"],"forum":"584sSv8CJk","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":521476,"cdate":1546300800000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747574242188,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"584sSv8CJk","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2024"},"pdf":{"value":"http://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.14398v2"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/journals/CORR/2024"},"paperhash":{"value":"kurz|languagespecific_calibration_for_pruning_multilingual_language_models"},"authorids":{"value":["~Simon_Kurz1","~Jian-Jia_Chen1","~Lucie_Flek1","~Zhixue_Zhao2"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.14398"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2408-14398,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={Simon Kurz and Jian-Jia Chen and Lucie Flek and Zhixue Zhao},\n  title={Language-specific Calibration for Pruning Multilingual Language Models},\n  year={2024},\n  cdate={1704067200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2408.14398},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2408.14398}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Recent advances in large language model (LLM) pruning have shown state-of-the-art compression results in post-training and retraining-free settings while maintaining high predictive performance. However, such research mainly considers calibrating pruning using English text, despite the multilingual nature of modern LLMs and their frequent uses in non-English languages. In this paper, we set out to explore effective strategies for calibrating the pruning of multilingual language models. We present the first comprehensive empirical study, comparing different calibration languages for pruning multilingual models across diverse tasks, models, and state-of-the-art pruning techniques. Our results present practical suggestions, for example, calibrating in the target language can efficiently yield lower perplexity, but does not necessarily benefit downstream tasks. Our further analysis experiments unveil that calibration in the target language mainly contributes to preserving language-specific features related to fluency and coherence, but might not contribute to capturing language-agnostic features such as language understanding and reasoning. Last, we provide practical recommendations for future practitioners."},"title":{"value":"Language-specific Calibration for Pruning Multilingual Language Models"},"authors":{"value":["Simon Kurz","Jian-Jia Chen","Lucie Flek","Zhixue Zhao"]}},"tmdate":1747831457648,"pdate":1704067200000,"tcdate":1727703389629,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~ZHIXUE_ZHAO1"],"forum":"0rt2Hqp1BX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":115824,"cdate":1704067200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit","DBLP.org/-/Author_Coreference"],"mdate":1747831457648,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"0rt2Hqp1BX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"CoRR 2025"},"pdf":{"value":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2512.24560v1"},"venueid":{"value":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article"},"paperhash":{"value":"gros|localized_calibrated_uncertainty_in_code_language_models"},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.24560"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@article{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2512-24560,\n  publtype={informal},\n  author={David Gros and Prem Devanbu},\n  title={Localized Calibrated Uncertainty in Code Language Models},\n  year={2025},\n  month={December},\n  cdate={1764547200000},\n  journal={CoRR},\n  volume={abs/2512.24560},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2512.24560}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"Large Language models (LLMs) can generate complicated source code from natural language prompts. However, LLMs can generate output that deviates from what the user wants, requiring supervision and editing. To support this process, we offer techniques to localize where generations might be misaligned from user intent. We first create a dataset of \"Minimal Intent Aligning Patches\" of repaired LLM generated programs. Each program uses test cases to verify correctness. After creating a dataset of programs, we measure how well various techniques can assign a well-calibrated probability to indicate which parts of code will be edited in a minimal patch (i.e., give a probability that corresponds with empirical odds it is edited). We compare white-box probing (where we propose a technique for efficient arbitrary-span querying), against black-box reflective and self-consistency based approaches. We find probes with a small supervisor model can achieve low calibration error and Brier Skill Score of approx 0.2 estimating edited lines on code generated by models many orders of magnitude larger. We discuss the generalizability of the techniques, and the connections to AI oversight and control, finding a probe trained only on code shows some signs of generalizing to natural language errors if new probability scaling is allowed."},"title":{"value":"Localized Calibrated Uncertainty in Code Language Models"},"authors":{"value":[{"fullname":"David Gros","username":"~David_Gros1"},{"fullname":"Prem Devanbu","username":""}]}},"tmdate":1788399901570,"pdate":1767139200000,"externalIds":["dblp:journals/corr/abs-2512-24560"],"tcdate":1788399890652,"writers":["~","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org","OpenReview.net/Support"],"signatures":["~David_Gros1"],"forum":"ay6ndWhNnQ","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":146362,"cdate":1764547200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["OpenReview.net/Public_Article/DBLP.org/-/Record","OpenReview.net/Public_Article/-/Edit"],"mdate":1788399901570,"domain":"OpenReview.net/Public_Article","id":"ay6ndWhNnQ","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MoDELS 2022"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2022"},"paperhash":{"value":"baresi|a_declarative_modelling_framework_for_the_deployment_and_management_of_blockchain_applications"},"authorids":{"value":["~Luciano_Baresi1","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Giovanni_Quattrocchi:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Damian_Andrew_Tamburri:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Luca_Terracciano:"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3550355.3552417"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/BaresiQTT22,\n  author={Luciano Baresi and Giovanni Quattrocchi and Damian Andrew Tamburri and Luca Terracciano},\n  title={A declarative modelling framework for the deployment and management of blockchain applications},\n  year={2022},\n  cdate={1640995200000},\n  pages={311-321},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1145/3550355.3552417},\n  booktitle={MoDELS},\n  crossref={conf/models/2022}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"The deployment and management of Blockchain applications require non-trivial efforts given the unique characteristics of their infrastructure (i.e., immutability) and the complexity of the software systems being executed. The operation of Blockchain applications is still based on ad-hoc solutions that are error-prone, difficult to maintain and evolve, and do not manage their interactions with other infrastructures (e.g., a Cloud backend).This paper proposes KATENA, a framework for the deployment and management of Blockchain applications. In particular, it focuses on applications that are compatible with Ethereum, a popular general-purpose Blockchain technology. KATENA provides i) a metamodel for defining Blockchain applications, ii) a set of processes to automate the deployment and management of defined models, and iii) an implementation of the approach based on TOSCA, a standard language for Infrastructure-as-Code, and xOpera, a TOSCA-compatible orchestrator. To evaluate the approach, we applied KATENA to model and deploy three real-world Blockchain applications, and showed that our solution reduces the amount of code required for their operations up to 82.7%."},"title":{"value":"A declarative modelling framework for the deployment and management of blockchain applications"},"authors":{"value":["Luciano Baresi","Giovanni Quattrocchi","Damian Andrew Tamburri","Luca Terracciano"]}},"tmdate":1758635165740,"pdate":1640995200000,"externalIds":["dblp:conf/models/BaresiQTT22"],"tcdate":1758635151585,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Luciano_Baresi1"],"forum":"d5EX97JphX","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":627229,"cdate":1640995200000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1758635165740,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"d5EX97JphX","version":2},{"content":{"venue":{"value":"MoDELS 2012"},"pdf":{"value":"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-642-33666-9_28.pdf"},"venueid":{"value":"dblp.org/conf/MODELS/2012"},"paperhash":{"value":"büttner|on_verifying_atl_transformations_using_offtheshelf_smt_solvers"},"authorids":{"value":["https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Fabian_Büttner:","https://dblp.org/search/pid/api?q=author:Marina_Egea:","~Jordi_Cabot1"]},"html":{"value":"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-33666-9_28"},"_bibtex":{"value":"@inproceedings{DBLP:conf/models/ButtnerEC12,\n  author={Fabian Büttner and Marina Egea and Jordi Cabot},\n  title={On Verifying ATL Transformations Using 'off-the-shelf' SMT Solvers},\n  year={2012},\n  cdate={1325376000000},\n  pages={432-448},\n  url={https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-33666-9_28},\n  booktitle={MoDELS},\n  crossref={conf/models/2012}\n}\n"},"abstract":{"value":"MDE is a software development process where models constitute pivotal elements of the software to be built. If models are well-specified, transformations can be employed for various purposes, e.g., to produce final code. However, transformations are only meaningful when they are ‘correct’: they must produce valid models from valid input models. A valid model has conformance to its meta-model and fulfils its constraints, usually written in OCL. In this paper, we propose a novel methodology to perform automatic, unbounded verification of ATL transformations. Its main component is a novel first-order semantics for ATL transformations, based on the interpretation of the corresponding rules and their execution semantics as first-order predicates. Although, our semantics is not complete, it does cover a significant subset of the ATL language. Using this semantics, transformation correctness can be automatically verified with respect to non-trivial OCL pre- and postconditions by using SMT solvers, e.g. Z3 and Yices."},"title":{"value":"On Verifying ATL Transformations Using 'off-the-shelf' SMT Solvers"},"authors":{"value":["Fabian Büttner","Marina Egea","Jordi Cabot"]}},"tmdate":1747574267741,"pdate":1325376000000,"tcdate":1747574217243,"writers":["~"],"signatures":["~Jordi_Cabot1"],"forum":"xLGEjKAlDW","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","number":521582,"cdate":1325376000000,"readers":["everyone"],"invitations":["DBLP.org/-/Record","DBLP.org/-/Edit"],"mdate":1747574267741,"domain":"DBLP.org","id":"xLGEjKAlDW","version":2}],"count":10000}